# Unified Theory of Epistemic Engineering (v3)

> **Validation status:** This is a synthesis framework, not an empirically validated causal model. Its primary value is *triage* — helping a team decide whether a problem belongs to AI, where on the capability/inspectability tradeoff a system should sit, and where tensions will accumulate. Its secondary value is *diagnostic* — explaining why a built system is failing. Predictive claims are flagged in [Section 11](#11-open-claims--validation-status).

> **Audience:** This framework is most useful for teams building AI systems in domains where individual decisions must be defensible to adversarial post-hoc scrutiny — engineering bureaucracies (NASA, aerospace, infrastructure), high-capital-risk environments (regulated financial services, energy, defence), and legal or regulatory bureaucracies (courts, healthcare, insurance, government adjudication). Teams in capability-end domains (consumer recommendations, creative tools, internal productivity assistants) will find parts of the framework useful but its heavier machinery is over-engineered for their problems.

---

## 1. Core Thesis

AI implementation is not primarily a software problem. It is an **Epistemic Engineering** problem.

The central act of epistemic engineering is choosing — explicitly and defensibly — where a system sits on the **capability/inspectability tradeoff**, and then engineering the attestation surfaces that make that choice operational.

Larger, more agentic, more integrated AI systems are more capable. They are also less inspectable, because their capability comes precisely from the integration that defeats decomposition. This is not a temporary engineering limitation that better AI will eventually erase. It is a structural tension. Capability and inspectability trade against each other in a continuous curve, and managing that curve — not pretending it doesn't exist — is the work.

In domains where institutional trust depends on the ability to defend individual decisions to outside scrutiny, the tradeoff must be navigated toward the inspectability end. In domains where trust depends on aggregate performance and individual decisions are not adjudicated, the tradeoff can be navigated toward the capability end. The technology does not determine the right position; the domain's normative requirements do.

Most candidate AI projects either fail in production or never should have been started. The common root cause is a mismatch between three things — the **Territory** (what the business reality actually is), the **Map** (how data represents that reality), and the **View** (what the AI projects as output) — compounded by an unexamined or wrong position on the capability/inspectability curve. This framework's primary job is to surface those misalignments and that tradeoff *before* a system is built. Its secondary job is to diagnose them when they emerge in a system that was built anyway.

