## 2. The Capability / Inspectability Tradeoff

This is the framework's organising principle. Every architectural choice in an AI system can be located as a point on a continuous curve, with one end optimised for capability, flexibility, speed, and convenience, and the other end optimised for inspectability, attestation, auditability, and per-decision accountability.

### The Curve

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Capability End<br/>Larger models<br/>Agentic loops<br/>Integrated reasoning<br/>Dynamic edges<br/>Output-only attestation"]
    B["Inspectability End<br/>Decomposed pipelines<br/>Verifier nodes<br/>Externalised stages<br/>Deterministic edges<br/>Per-stage attestation"]

    A <-->|"continuous tradeoff"| B

    classDef cap fill:#FCA5A5,stroke:#B91C1C,color:#111111;
    classDef ins fill:#A7F3D0,stroke:#047857,color:#111111;

    class A cap;
    class B ins;
```

Within a region of the curve, architectural choices tune the system smoothly. A team can externalise one more stage, add one more verifier, accept one more attestation latency cost, and the system slides along the curve in proportion. The framework's vocabulary — Stages, Nodes, Edges, Attestation surfaces, all introduced below — is the toolkit for navigating this continuous region.

A footnote on geometry. The 2D curve is a teaching device. The actual tradeoff is multi-dimensional: capability, inspectability, latency, cost, and operational complexity all interact. A 2D rendering loses these dimensions but preserves the most important relationship — the structural tension between integration and decomposition — and a higher-dimensional rendering would be unteachable. Sophisticated readers should treat the curve as a projection of a hyperplane onto the two dimensions that matter most for the framework's purposes.

### The Phase Shift

Somewhere along the curve, the regime changes. Below a threshold, the system is run as a **governance contract** — the institution must be able to defend individual decisions, so attestation surfaces are non-negotiable and the framework's full machinery applies. Above the threshold, the system is run as a **capability artefact** — trust is established through aggregate performance, not per-decision defensibility, and the framework's heavier machinery would be over-engineering.

The phase shift is not a point where teams "decide to care less about inspectability." It is where the *form* of trust changes from per-decision attestation to aggregate-outcome trust. These are different kinds of trust, not less of the same kind. A team that crosses the threshold without realising they have crossed it has typically committed an institutional error: deploying a system that operates as a capability artefact in a context that demands a governance contract.

### Locating the Phase Shift: Three Signals

The phase shift sits roughly at the boundary between domains that require per-decision defensibility and domains that tolerate aggregate-outcome trust. Three signals locate it:

**Signal 1: Individuated vs aggregated decisions.** Domains that adjudicate individual cases — medical diagnosis, legal advice, sentencing, loan approval, insurance claims, regulatory adjudication, mission-critical engineering decisions — require per-decision attestation because the individual or institution affected has standing to challenge a specific decision. Domains that operate on populations — search ranking, content recommendation, ad targeting, marketing personalisation — tolerate aggregate-outcome trust because no individual case has standing to demand justification.

**Signal 2: Adversarial post-hoc scrutiny.** Will the institution have to reconstruct, after a failure, exactly why a specific decision was made, who was responsible, and whether the process was defensible? NASA after a launch anomaly, a bank after a trading loss, a court after an appeal, a hospital after a malpractice claim, a regulator after an enforcement action — all face this requirement. A consumer app does not. The presence of adversarial post-hoc reconstruction is the strongest single signal that a domain sits below the phase shift.

**Signal 3: Reversibility of decisions.** A misrouted ticket is recoverable. A misdiagnosed cancer, a mis-launched rocket, a mis-sentenced defendant, a mis-approved loan that triggers cascading exposure — these are not. Irreversibility shifts a domain toward the inspectability end because the cost of failure cannot be absorbed by aggregate performance.

Domains that score strongly on all three signals — engineering bureaucracies like NASA, high-capital-risk environments like investment banking and infrastructure, legal and regulatory bureaucracies — sit unambiguously below the phase shift. They are the framework's primary audience. Domains that score weakly on all three sit above the shift; the framework's heavier machinery is not for them. The interesting cases are domains that score on some signals but not others, and the framework has the most to say about navigating these.

### Phase-Shift Drift

The framework names a primary failure mode of AI adoption in inspectability-end domains: the gravitational pull toward the capability end. The drift is real, it is economically rational at the level of the individual decision-maker (who benefits from speed, flexibility, and convenience), and it is institutionally catastrophic when it accumulates in a domain that structurally requires per-decision attestation.

Drift typically manifests as a sequence of locally reasonable decisions: a more capable model swapped in for an interpretable one because evals improved; stages integrated more tightly because latency mattered for a demo; a Dynamic Edge accepted where a Deterministic Edge was originally specified because the contract felt too restrictive in practice; verifier nodes deferred to a later release because they slowed the build. Each step looks defensible. The cumulative drift is a system that no longer meets its domain's normative requirements, with no single responsible decision to roll back.

The framework's warning: in inspectability-end domains, drift toward the capability end should be treated as a first-class risk and tracked explicitly. Architecture review should include a periodic re-examination of where the system actually sits on the tradeoff curve, compared with where it was specified to sit, and what conditions (regulatory, institutional, normative) are anchoring the placement.

