## 4. The Three-Layer Stack

This framework rests on three bodies of work. Each layer is not independent — each one constrains the one below it.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    L1["Layer 1: Epistemic Discipline<br/>Do not collapse Territory, Map, and View"]
    L2["Layer 2: IR Design Space<br/>Choose representations and retrieval deliberately"]
    L3["Layer 3: FCTAF<br/>Assign nodes and edge contracts in production"]
    P["Production AI System<br/>Managed information supply chain"]

    L1 -->|"sets obligation"| L2
    L2 -->|"specifies representation choices"| L3
    L3 -->|"manages loss across stages"| P

    M1["Without Layer 1:<br/>tool confused with task"]
    M2["Without Layer 2:<br/>loss profile chosen accidentally"]
    M3["Without Layer 3:<br/>no pipeline accountability"]

    L1 -.-> M1
    L2 -.-> M2
    L3 -.-> M3

    classDef layer fill:#D8B4FE,stroke:#6B21A8,color:#111111;
    classDef system fill:#A7F3D0,stroke:#047857,color:#111111;
    classDef warning fill:#FCA5A5,stroke:#B91C1C,color:#111111;

    class L1,L2,L3 layer;
    class P system;
    class M1,M2,M3 warning;
```

**Layer 1 — Epistemic Discipline** governs conceptual hygiene. It establishes the obligation to keep Territory, Map, and View distinct, to acknowledge that every representation involves deliberate loss, and to maintain the directionality of the pragmatic→semantic→syntactic chain. Without this layer, teams conflate the tool with the task and cannot explain why a well-built system keeps producing wrong answers.

**Layer 2 — IR Design Space** provides the technical vocabulary for making representation decisions explicit. It maps the *Data Landscape* (structured, semi-structured, entropic) against the *Query Landscape* (Fact, Procedural, Analytical, Contextual) to specify which representation technique (Sparse, Dense, Graph, Summarised) and retrieval technique (Lexical, Semantic, Hybrid, Generative) is appropriate for the task.

**Layer 3 — FCTAF** operationalises the pipeline. It breaks the task into five discrete stages, assigns Node types to each, and defines Edge contracts between stages. It provides the structure for managing information loss across the supply chain — and for the attestation surfaces that make stage-level oversight possible.

### The Causal Chain

Layer 1 tells you **what not to collapse:** do not conflate the Map with the Territory; do not conflate the View with the Map; do not invert the pragmatic→semantic→syntactic order.

Layer 2 tells you **how to represent:** given that every representation is a structured loss, which losses are acceptable? What representation preserves what the Query Landscape requires?

Layer 3 tells you **who manages each loss and which stages must be externalised for attestation:** which Node type owns each stage, which Edges enforce contracts, which transitions require attestation surfaces given the system's position on the capability/inspectability curve.

