## 12. Open Claims / Validation Status

| Claim | Status | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Pipeline decomposition (5 stages) | Grounded | Consistent with Parasuraman (2000), Endsley (1995), CTA literature. Stage boundaries are novel. |
| Pragmatic / Semantic / Syntactic trichotomy | Grounded | Morris (1938); standard in linguistics and KR. Mapping to FCTAF stages is novel. |
| Loss-propagation directionality | Theoretically grounded | Follows from the trichotomy's level structure. Empirical confirmation would require failure-mode studies. |
| **Capability / Inspectability tradeoff curve** | Novel | The framework's central organising claim. Empirically supported by industry experience but not formally validated. |
| **Phase shift in the tradeoff curve** | Novel | Distinguishes governance-contract from capability-artefact regimes. The location of the shift varies by domain and is operationalised by the three signals. |
| **Three signals for locating the phase shift** | Novel | Individuated decisions, adversarial post-hoc scrutiny, irreversibility. Claim that these jointly locate the threshold is theoretically motivated but unvalidated. |
| **Phase-shift drift as primary failure mode** | Novel | Plausible from observed industry patterns; not empirically established. |
| Node taxonomy (AI / Code / Human + AI subtypes) | Partially grounded | AI/Code/Human simplifies Sheridan & Verplank (1978); subtype taxonomy is novel. |
| Edge taxonomy (Deterministic / Dynamic / Feedback) | Theoretical | Novel. Edges as attestation surfaces is a further claim. |
| **Attestation surface taxonomy (Logged / Automated / Human)** | Partially grounded | Standard in safety-critical software (DO-178C, AMLAS); novel in AI-engineering frameworks. |
| Synthesis Depth as difficulty lever | Grounded | Wei et al. (2022) CoT evidence. D∞ category is novel. |
| Epistemic Friction as lever | Theoretical | Pirolli & Card (1999). AI-specific cost mapping unvalidated. |
| Intent Variance as lever | Theoretical | Standard in HCI; partition is the framework's. |
| Consequence Asymmetry as lever | Novel | Required for triage; not validated against production failure data. |
| **Mission-Criticality as separate dimension** | Novel | Distinct from consequence; validated by the existence of regulated domains where consequences are recoverable but oversight requirements are absolute. |
| Lever interaction effects (multiplicative) | Speculative | Plausible but not empirically established. |
| Solvability ceiling claim | Speculative | The claim that some five-tuples exceed current AI capability is the framework's strongest pre-build claim and the least empirically grounded. |
| Hallucination as Stage IV failure (with model-internal subtype) | Well-documented | RAGAS (Es 2023), Huang (2023). Upstream-cause framing is novel. |
| Stage I–II failures dominate production | Speculative | Plausible from RAG literature (Gao 2023) but no systematic production study exists. |
| Territory / Map / View failure taxonomy | Novel | Synthesised from prior work. No direct prior art in AI engineering. |
| Interface vs internal stage distinction | Novel | Tracks observability of modern AI systems; falsifiable as interpretability matures. |

**What would validate the framework:**

1. A large-scale study of production AI system failures categorised by FCTAF stage, edge-type, and attestation-surface presence.
2. Retrospective application of the Suitability Assessment to abandoned or failed AI projects, testing whether the verdict and tension forecast would have predicted failure.
3. Empirical work on the phase shift: do domains scoring strongly on the three signals actually produce different failure profiles than domains scoring weakly?
4. Studies of phase-shift drift in deployed systems: does the predicted gravitational pull toward capability-end choices manifest in real organisational settings?
5. Connections to existing assurance-case frameworks (DO-178C, ISO 26262, AMLAS) — does the framework's vocabulary translate cleanly into existing safety-critical software practice?

