## 13. How to Use This Document

**For a team starting an AI project in an inspectability-end domain:** read Sections 1–2 for the central tradeoff, Section 3 for vocabulary, then Sections 6 and 7 to characterise the problem and assess suitability. Sections 8–10 become relevant only if the assessment returns Yes or Yes-with-conditions and you are building.

**For a team debugging a built system:** read Sections 1–5 for vocabulary and loss propagation, then Section 10 for the diagnostic protocol. Section 11's worked example shows how the protocol traces a symptomatic failure back to its primary cause. Pay particular attention to the phase-shift drift question (Section 10, item 10) — many production failures in regulated contexts are drift failures rather than build failures.

**For a team in a capability-end domain:** Sections 1–5 give vocabulary that may be useful. Sections 6–10 are over-engineered for your context. The framework's main message for you is the phase shift itself: know which side of it you're on, and resist organisational pressure to apply your design patterns to colleagues working on the other side.

**For a team operating near the phase shift:** the framework is most useful and most contested in your context. The three signals in Section 2 should be applied carefully; if any of them score strongly, default to the inspectability side. Drift is the largest risk; instrument for it explicitly.

The framework's claims are layered. Sections 1–5 are the most stable; Sections 6–7 contain the strongest and least-validated claims and are where the framework most needs empirical work; Sections 8–10 are operational and largely inherit from established practice. Section 12 is honest about which is which.

